# An arrow key is not a request for a focus ring.
#
# The rule used to arm on any of Tab/Up/Down/Left/Right, because all five look
# like navigation. In hanabi none of the four arrows moves the ring's focus and
# each already has an indicator of its own: preload.cpp sets arrows_tab = false
# so process_tabbing never sees them, a list arrow walks MainPaneSystem's own
# keyboard cursor (which draws the hover surface plus an accent border on the
# row), and an arrow in a text field walks the caret.
#
# So the ring an arrow armed was a ring around a widget the arrow had not gone
# to. Pressing Left to move the caret one character in the composer put a
# second outline around the field, on top of the focused border the composer
# draws for itself: two rings on one control, out of register, for a keystroke
# that moved a caret.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":["t2"],"active_tab":"t2","theme":"dark"}
# env: HANABI_FOCUS_AUDIT=1

# A click puts the ring away and puts the caret in the composer.
click_ui composer_reply_input
wait_frames 6
type "hello"
wait_frames 6
expect_text "ring off"

# Left moves the caret. The field's own focused border is the indicator here,
# and it is already drawn — nothing about this keystroke asks for a ring.
key LEFT
wait_frames 8
expect_text "ring off"

key RIGHT
wait_frames 8
expect_text "ring off"

# Up and Down in the composer walk the send history, which is the same story.
key UP
wait_frames 8
expect_text "ring off"

# Tab still arms it: the rule narrowed, it did not go away.
key TAB
wait_frames 8
expect_text "ring on"
